WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
How can a tree or butterfly act on time without a calendar?

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.380
Watch one dial carry a day inside a year.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.920
Its blue light band turns.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.620
Its red temperature band rises and falls.

5
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.460
The living things change with it.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.220
Start with the inner dial.

7
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.380
A circadian rhythm repeats about once each day.

8
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.260
Light and darkness help keep many rhythms aligned.

9
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.760
Many owls and many bats hunt at night.

10
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.800
Tree squirrels and most songbirds are active by day.

11
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.820
Yet the rhythm is not simply a reaction to each sunrise.

12
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.333
Here is your prediction.

13
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:47.733
The blue light band disappears, leaving steady darkness.

14
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:53.253
Will the gold activity rhythm vanish at once, or keep circling for a while?

15
00:00:54.573 --> 00:00:56.193
Watch the gold marker carefully.

16
00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:58.873
Choose before the dial moves.

17
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.710
In many animals, it keeps circling for a while.

18
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.930
That tells us some timing comes from inside the body.

19
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.550
Outside light still matters because it keeps that inner rhythm lined up with the

20
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.430
day. Inner timing and outside light work together.

21
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:24.593
Not every response is a rhythm.

22
00:01:25.913 --> 00:01:28.953
A tropism is plant growth toward or away from something.

23
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:31.933
Light moves beside a bean shoot.

24
00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:37.193
Cells lengthen more on the shaded side, so new growth bends toward light.

25
00:01:38.813 --> 00:01:40.613
Roots can also curve toward wetter places.

26
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:43.557
Now widen back to the year.

27
00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:48.297
Shorter days and cooler air can time seasonal responses.

28
00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:53.317
Monarchs migrate. Tree buds become dormant.

29
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:59.097
Groundhogs hibernate, lowering body temperature and using less stored energy.

30
00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:03.697
Deciduous trees also drop leaves as winter approaches.

31
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.180
These responses meet a hard season in different ways.

32
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.820
Migration changes place.

33
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.660
Dormancy slows growth while life continues.

34
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.780
Hibernation is animal dormancy, not one unbroken sleep.

35
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.460
Groundhogs briefly warm, then cool again.

36
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.880
Each response helps the organism meet hard conditions.

37
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:31.323
Outside cues are not the whole story.

38
00:02:32.603 --> 00:02:35.043
Some animals also have inner yearly timing.

39
00:02:36.343 --> 00:02:41.583
With day length held steady, groundhog eating and weight cycles kept repeating about

40
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:45.783
once a year. Many seasonal behaviors are inherited.

41
00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:51.803
A first-time monarch can fly south without being taught, although some animals do

42
00:02:51.803 --> 00:02:52.803
learn routes.

43
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.487
Species combine cues differently.

44
00:02:56.907 --> 00:03:01.207
Many tree buds need winter cold before later warmth can open them.

45
00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:03.967
The order of cues can matter.

46
00:03:05.207 --> 00:03:08.207
So one warm day is not a universal start button.

47
00:03:09.507 --> 00:03:12.447
The response depends on the organism's timing system.

48
00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:15.827
The picture now has two kinds of timing.

49
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:18.707
Gold inner rhythms keep turning.

50
00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:23.387
Blue and red cues help line up responses with the world.

51
00:03:24.647 --> 00:03:27.247
No single cue controls every organism.

52
00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:32.967
Across days and seasons, the useful response arrives at a useful time.
